SELECTION CRITERIA:
We included studies if they were conducted as a randomised controlled trial (RCT), involving adults (≥ 18 years) with a 
diagnosis of HF. We included trials comparing mHealth-delivered education such as internet and web-based education 
programmes for use on smartphones and tablets (including apps) and other mobile devices, SMS messages and social 
media-delivered education programmes, versus usual HF care.

STUDY TITLE:
The role of home-based information and communications technology interventions in chronic disease management: a 
systematic literature review

STUDY ABSTRACT:
This article presents a systematic literature review done to evaluate the feasibility and benefits of home-based 
information and communications technology enabled interventions for chronic disease management, with emphasis on their 
impact on health outcomes and costs. Relevant articles were retrieved from PubMed and evaluated using quality worksheets
with pre-identified inclusion and exclusion criteria. Of the 256 articles retrieved, 27 were found to concord with the 
study criteria. Evaluation of the identified articles was conducted irrespective of study design, type of home-based 
intervention or chronic disease involved. The review demonstrates that HBIs applied to chronic disease management 
improve functional and cognitive patient outcomes and reduce healthcare spending. However, further research is needed to
assess benefit in terms of evidence-based outcome indicators (that can provide a basis for meta-analysis), to confirm 
sustainable cost benefits, and to systematically collect data on physician satisfaction with patient management.

INSTRUCTIONS: Based on the selection criteria above, decide whether this study should be INCLUDED or EXCLUDED. Provide a
brief rationale tied to specific criteria.


