Execution logs:
Study Title: Outcomes of a telehealth intervention for homebound older adults with heart or chronic respiratory failure:
a randomized controlled trial
Study Abstract: PURPOSE  Telehealth care is emerging as a viable intervention model to treat complex chronic conditions,
such as heart failure (HF) and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), and to engage older adults in self-care 
disease management.  DESIGN AND METHODS  We report on a randomized controlled trial examining the impact of a 
multifaceted telehealth intervention on health, mental health, and service utilization outcomes among homebound 
medically ill older adults diagnosed with HF or COPD. Random effects regression modeling was used, and we hypothesized 
that older adults in the telehealth intervention (n = 51) would receive significantly better quality of care resulting 
in improved scores in health-related quality of life, mental health, and satisfaction with care at 3 months follow-up as
compared with controls (n = 51) and service utilization outcomes at 12 months follow-up.  RESULTS  At follow-up, the 
telehealth intervention group reported greater increases in general health and social functioning, and improved in 
depression symptom scores as compared with usual care plus education group. The control group had significantly more 
visits to the emergency department than the telehealth group. There was an observed trend toward fewer hospital days for
telehealth participants, but it did not reach significance at 12 months.  IMPLICATIONS  Telehealth may be an efficient 
and effective method of systematically delivering integrated care in the home health sector. The use of telehealth 
technology may benefit homebound older adults who have difficulty accessing care due to disability, transportation, or 
isolation.
Selection Criteria: We included studies if they were conducted as a randomised controlled trial (RCT), involving adults 
(≥ 18 years) with a diagnosis of HF. We included trials comparing mHealth‐delivered education such as internet and 
web‐based education programmes for use on smartphones and tablets (including apps) and other mobile devices, SMS 
messages and social media‐delivered education programmes, versus usual HF care.
Study Index: 32

Screening Tool Output:
=== SCREENING TASK: Study 32 ===

SELECTION CRITERIA:
We included studies if they were conducted as a randomised controlled trial (RCT), involving adults (≥ 18 years) with a 
diagnosis of HF. We included trials comparing mHealth‐delivered education such as internet and web‐based education 
programmes for use on smartphones and tablets (including apps) and other mobile devices, SMS messages and social 
media‐delivered education programmes, versus usual HF care.

STUDY TITLE:
Outcomes of a telehealth intervention for homebound older adults with heart or chronic respiratory failure: a randomized
controlled trial

STUDY ABSTRACT:
PURPOSE  Telehealth care is emerging as a viable intervention model to treat complex chronic conditions, such as heart 
failure (HF) and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), and to engage older adults in self-care disease 
management.  DESIGN AND METHODS  We report on a randomized controlled trial examining the impact of a multifaceted 
telehealth intervention on health, mental health, and service utilization outcomes among homebound medically ill older 
adults diagnosed with HF or COPD. Random effects regression modeling was used, and we hypothesized that older adults in 
the telehealth intervention (n = 51) would receive significantly better quality of care resulting in improved scores in 
health-related quality of life, mental health, and satisfaction with care at 3 months follow-up as compared with 
controls (n = 51) and service utilization outcomes at 12 months follow-up.  RESULTS  At follow-up, the telehealth 
intervention group reported greater increases in general health and social functioning, and improved in depression 
symptom scores as compared with usual care plus education group. The control group had significantly more visits to the 
emergency department than the telehealth group. There was an observed trend toward fewer hospital days for telehealth 
participants, but it did not reach significance at 12 months.  IMPLICATIONS  Telehealth may be an efficient and 
effective method of systematically delivering integrated care in the home health sector. The use of telehealth 
technology may benefit homebound older adults who have difficulty accessing care due to disability, transportation, or 
isolation.

