STUDY TITLE:
Home telemonitoring to improve disease management and clinical outcomes in patients with heart failure: a systematic 
review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials

STUDY ABSTRACT:
BackgroundPrevious published studies on home telemetry have reported inconsistent results and current published 
systematic reviews have not included recent trials or were unable to make definite conclusions because of the paucity of
relevant studies. The medical literature was systematically reviewed for the most recent clinical trials to summarize 
the effect of home telemonitoring strategies on disease management and clinical outcomes in patients with congestive HF 
in the year 2015.MethodsA search in Medline and the Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials (from January 1995 to
December 2014) was performed to identify randomized controlled studies designed to evaluate the effect of home 
telemonitoring on the management and clinical outcome of patients with chronic HF. For the clinical endpoints of 
mortality and hospital readmission for HF, we calculated odds ratios with Woolf 95% confidence intervals using 
Mantel-Haenszel fixed effects. A chi-square test was used to assess heterogeneity among the included studies. The degree
of heterogeneity was evaluated by the I2 index. Systemic bias, including publication bias was assessed by examining the 
funnel plots for asymmetry.ResultsIn total, 3,968 patients were randomized, of whom 2,002 were allocated to home 
telemonitoring (HTM) and 1,966 patients to control. The time of follow-up ranged from 3 months to 1 year. Mortality 
occurred in 10.4% patients with HTM and in 12.8% patients in the control group (OR 0.81, 95% CI 0.64-1.01, p= 0.06, I2= 
0%). Hospital readmission occurred in 22.9% patients with HTM and in 25.4% patients in the control group (OR 0.89, 95% 
CI 0.76-1.04, p= 0.15, I2= 58%). The funnel plot revealed a symmetrical pattern for studies evaluating all-cause 
mortality, suggesting that publication bias was unlikely and an asymmetrical pattern for studies evaluating hospital 
admission, suggesting publication bias may have occurred.ConclusionsAlthough home telemonitoring has lost favor as a 
promising non-invasive strategy to intensify and improve HF management in the outpatient setting, its role may be under 
utilized in the patient-centered home, with potential to reduce all-cause mortality and hospitalization for HF.jacc.org

