0.49, 95% confidence interval (CI) = 0.27-0.91, P = 0.02] and heart failure-related re-hospitalizations (HR = 0.44, 95% 
CI = 0.25-0.77, P = 0.004). MDP was still associated with better outcomes for all-cause death (HR = 0.53, 95% CI =      
0.29-0.98, P = 0.04) and heart failure-related re-hospitalization (HR = 0.46, 95% CI = 0.26-0.81, P = 0.007), after     
adjusting for age, diuretics, diabetes mellitus, chronic kidney disease, hypertension, sodium, and albumin. However,    
MDPs' effect on all-cause mortality and heart failure-related re-hospitalization was significantly attenuated after     
adjusting for angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors/angiotensin II receptor blockers or β-blockers. A stratified     
analysis showed that MDP combined with guideline-based medication had synergistic effects. CONCLUSIONS MDP is effective 
in lowering all-cause mortality and re-hospitalization rates related to heart failure under a national health insurance 
programme. MDP synergistically improves the effectiveness of guidelines-based medications for heart failure."""         
