screen_study output: === SCREENING TASK: Study 154 ===

SELECTION CRITERIA:
We included studies if they were conducted as a randomised controlled trial (RCT), involving adults (≥ 18 years) with a 
diagnosis of HF. We included trials comparing mHealth‐delivered education such as internet and web‐based education 
programmes for use on smartphones and tablets (including apps) and other mobile devices, SMS messages and social 
media‐delivered education programmes, versus usual HF care.

STUDY TITLE:
Abstract 16050: feasibility and engagement with mhealth monitoring in rural-dwelling older adults with heart failure: a 
pilot study

STUDY ABSTRACT:
Introduction: Heart failure (HF) is associated with high rates of (re)hospitalizations, comorbidity, and mortality. 
Remote patient monitoring using mHealth equipment shows promise in improving self-care and reducing these tragic 
statistics, yet limited information exists regarding its use in rural-dwelling older adults. Purpose: We compared 
perspectives of older adults with HF who were randomized to either: 1) mHealth equipment linked to a 24-hour call 
center, 2) home equipment, or 3) standard care with regard to ease and satisfaction with equipment, self-care 
management, and provider communication and engagement. Methods: We used a mixed methods design with pre/post surveys of 
HF symptoms, equipment usability, technology anxiety, treatment adherence, health distress and semi-structured 
interviews regarding care engagement, communication, and technology. Results: We enrolled 28 HF patients >55 years; with
57% male, 79% White, and 43% had attended some college. Half had hospital visits this year. At baseline, 50% rated their
health fair or poor and 53% were discouraged by their health; 21% did not monitor their blood pressure, weight, or diet;
36% and 32% agreed that technology made them nervous and afraid. Fifty-seven percent believed it was better to seek 
professional help than caring for oneself and they reported high levels of provider engagement. Post-survey data at 
12-weeks showed significant improvement in self-monitoring, care perceptions and general health symptoms. The mHealth 
group had significant improvement in treatment adherence compared to the other groups. Narrative data revealed main 
themes: 1) home-monitoring/technology helpful; 2) communication and engagement with providers were good, but emergency 
department visits were routine; 3) persistent health problems; and 4) increased awareness and confidence with 
self-monitoring of symptoms. Conclusions: mHealth equipment is feasible with potential to improve outcomes and reduce 
(re)hospitalizations of rural-dwelling older adults with HF.Am Heart Assoc

INSTRUCTIONS: Based on the selection criteria above, decide whether this study should be INCLUDED or EXCLUDED. Provide a
brief rationale tied to specific criteria.


