Execution logs:
=== SCREENING TASK: Study 155 ===

SELECTION CRITERIA:
- Study design: randomised controlled trial (RCT)
- Population: adults (>=18 years) with a diagnosis of heart failure
- Intervention: mHealth-delivered education (mobile and wireless technologies to achieve health objectives) such as 
internet/web-based education programmes for smartphones/tablets (including apps), SMS messages, social media-delivered 
education programmes
- Comparison: usual HF care

STUDY TITLE:
Implementation of an e-health tool in heart failure clinics in primary care

STUDY ABSTRACT:
Introduction: Approximately 60% of patients with heart failure (HF) receive treatment in primary care (PC). To ensure 
evidence-based care through the chain of care, HF clinics in PC was established and an e-health tool, OPTILOGG, was 
tested in all PC centers in a region in Sweden. OPTILOGG is a homebased tool, consisting of a preprogramed tablet with a
weighing scale. It consists of three intervention modes, 1-interactive education, 2-symptom monitoring (weight and 
self-assessed levels of shortness of breath, fatigue and oedema), 3-support to perform flexible diuretic intake. 
OPTILOGG actively intervenes either by advising an increase in the dose of diuretics following a swift weight increase 
or by alerting the patient to contact the HF clinic. The aim was three-folded; 1. To describe patients’ adherence to 
using OPTILOGG, 2. To explore the numbers of alerts given by the OPTILOGG, 3. To evaluate the effects on patients’ 
self-care behavior. Methods: The study had an explorative pre-post design. Data regarding adherence and numbers of 
alerts was collected during 4 months. European Heart Failure Self-care Behaviour scale (EHFScB-9) were used to measure 
self-care behavior. The responses range from 1-5 with lower scores indicating better self-care behavior. Results: 32 
patients (31% female, mean age 64,7), were equipped with OPTILOGG. The median adherence to daily use was 94% [IQR: 
83-98%]. There was on average 1.4 alerts per patient, and OPTILOGG actively intervened in 76% of the patients. 53% of 
the patients had one or more episodes where the dose of diuretics was increased for two consecutive days. In 35% of the 
patients, the increased dose did not manage to return the weight to normal. 41% of the patients reported changes in 
symptoms of a magnitude that resulted in alerts. Only 12% of the patients had alerts both related to weight and to 
self-reported symptoms. Median EHFScB-9 at baseline was 28.5 [IQR: 25-35] and 18.0 [IQR: 13-27] after 4 months, p<0,05. 
Conclusions: OPTILOGG intervened in 3 out of 4 patients and significantly improved patients’ self-care behavior. 
Self-assessed symptoms and weight do not correlate well and thus it seems meaningful to use both in order to maximize 
the probability of detecting an imminent exacerbation.Am Heart Assoc

